{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Addendum: measured timings + cleanup/concurrency evidence\n\n## Repeated wall-clock for `test_cli_show_complete_or_erroring_under_write_burst`\n\n**Ubuntu (real CI job logs, Gerrit Verified Gate run 32296354188):**\n- ubuntu-latest py3.11 (job 96208832877): **12.79s**\n- ubuntu-latest (job 96208832933): **14.54s**\n- ubuntu-latest (job 96208832966): **14.93s**\n\n**macOS:**\n- macos-latest in CI (job 96208833105): **15.34s**\n- this host, 4 consecutive local runs: **8.76 / 8.25 / 8.40 / 8.17 s**\n\nCompanion generic tests in the same CI jobs: `..._promptly_while_write_lock_is_held`\n8.92-9.53s; `..._or_error_under_write_burst` 6.61-8.97s.\n\nThe measured Ubuntu samples (12.79-14.93s) sit inside the 10.95-22.63s range carried in\nfrom triage, confirming it.\n\n## Aggregate runner cost and savings\n\nThe suite runs on 4 pytest matrix jobs per change (3x ubuntu + 1x macos), so this single\ntest costs roughly **12.79 + 14.54 + 14.93 + 15.34 = 57.6s of runner time per change**.\n\n**Savings available from consolidation: 0s.** The matrix proves deleting this test forfeits\nthe F3/F6 partial-success detection class that no other oracle covers, so that 57.6s is not\nrecoverable by removal. Recovering it requires right-sizing the storm while keeping the\noracle — filed as `beribboned-spherical-pipit` (ce38-2914). Rough, UNMEASURED estimate for\nthat path: the storm is 48 CLI subprocesses (6 rounds x 4 tickets x edit+show); reducing to\nthe detection floor (F3/F6 fire on round 0) would cut most of the per-call cost, leaving\nfixture overhead — order ~40s/change recovered. That number is an estimate to be measured\nby that ticket, not a result of this investigation.\n\n## Process concurrency remains real (runtime-confirmed)\n\nSeed F1 was detected only by the tests that hold the tracker write lock in a live thread\nwhile a real `rebar` subprocess reads. Detection required genuine concurrency: the in-process\noracle measured a 15.1s stall on the held lock, and the CLI oracle observed its subprocess\nexceed the 10s bound. A mocked or serialized harness could not have produced either.\n\n## Process cleanup remains observable (runtime-confirmed)\n\nUnder seed F5 (a 35s stall inside `show`), the generic property's\n`except subprocess.TimeoutExpired` arm fired, the stalled subprocess was killed by the\n`timeout=30` bound, the `finally: release.set(); holder.join(...)` released the lock-holder\nthread, and the session terminated normally at 90.01s. No orphaned `rebar` processes remained\n(`pgrep` after the run showed only unrelated MCP/daemon processes).\n\nNote the asymmetry this exposes: the original test passes no `timeout` and so inherits the\nharness default of 60s, with no `except` arm — a stall there surfaces as a pytest ERROR, not\na targeted failure, and only above 60s. That is the mechanism behind F5's generic-only\ndetection.\n\n## Post-experiment state\n\nAll seven seeds reverted; `git status` clean at 0b851c8103. The unseeded pair is GREEN over\n3 consecutive verification runs (26.86-27.40s). **No test file and no production file was\nmodified by this investigation.**"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787172912071500001,"uuid":"6717b31c-7333-4f41-8976-133acf6f474c"}